-- title:
--   Equal-kernel homomorphisms to a field differ by a Frobenius power
-- statement:
--   Let $T$ be a commutative ring, $L$ a field, and $p$ a prime such that $L$ has characteristic $p$. Let $f_1, f_2 \colon T \to L$ be ring homomorphisms satisfying two hypotheses: their kernels coincide, $\ker f_1 = \ker f_2$, and the image $f_1(T) \subseteq L$ is a finite set. The conclusion asserts the existence of a natural number $j$ such that $f_2(t) = f_1(t)^{p^j}$ for every $t \in T$. Thus the two homomorphisms differ by the $j$-th power of the absolute Frobenius of $L$, a single exponent $j$ working simultaneously for all $t$. Note that finiteness is assumed only for the range of $f_1$; the conclusion forces the range of $f_2$ to be contained in it, and in fact equal to it.
--
--   This is the standard rigidity statement for two reductions of a ring to the same finite residue field: homomorphisms with the same kernel and finite image are related by a power of Frobenius, the Galois group of a finite field being generated by Frobenius. It is used to align two embeddings of one Hecke eigensystem into a residue field, in [`GaloisRep.exists_inertia_eigenvector_tameCharacter_pow_of_theta_heckeT_eq_zero_of_det_eq_pow_of_eq_two`](thm.html#GaloisRep.exists_inertia_eigenvector_tameCharacter_pow_of_theta_heckeT_eq_zero_of_det_eq_pow_of_eq_two).
-- source:
